<article class="env-proposition" id="06QB">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06QB">Proposition <span data-tag="06QB">9.5.2.2</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories, where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is presentable and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is locally small. Then $F$ admits a right adjoint if and only if it is cocontinuous: that is, it preserves small colimits. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume that $F$ is cocontinuous; we will show that it admits a right adjoint (the reverse implication follows from Corollary <a data-tag="02KE" href="/tag/02KE">7.1.4.28</a>). By virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="02J9" href="/tag/02J9">6.2.6.2</a>, it will suffice to show that for every functor $h_{D}: \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ which is representable by an object $D \in \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, the composition $(h_{D} \circ F^{\operatorname{op}}): \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ is representable by an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Using Theorem <a data-tag="06PY" href="/tag/06PY">9.5.1.8</a>, we are reduced to showing that the functor $h_{D} \circ F^{\operatorname{op}}$ preserves small limits. This follows from the cocontinuity of $F$, since $h_{D}$ preserves all limits which exist in $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}^{\operatorname{op}}$ (Corollary <a data-tag="039M" href="/tag/039M">7.4.1.23</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
